---
// /about (website-first-visit D3): who makes OpenTodo, since when, the licence in
// plain words, security, the specs, the demo, this website, and how to leave.
// Dates come from the bundle's changelog, the maintainer from authors.json.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EndCta from '../components/EndCta.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import DiscordButton from '../components/DiscordButton.astro';
import { manifest, releases, versionTag } from '../lib/bundle';
import { RELEASES_URL, SITE } from '../lib/site';
import authors from '../content/authors.json';

const maintainer = authors.raffaele;
const licence = manifest.facts.license ?? 'AGPL-3.0';
const day = (s: string) => new Date(`${s}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
// Oldest first: by date, then by version, since several releases can share a day.
const num = (v: string) => v.split(/[.-]/).map((x) => Number.parseInt(x, 10) || 0);
const byVersion = (a: string, b: string) => { const [x, y] = [num(a), num(b)]; for (let i = 0; i < Math.max(x.length, y.length); i++) if ((x[i] ?? 0) !== (y[i] ?? 0)) return (x[i] ?? 0) - (y[i] ?? 0); return 0; };
const sorted = [...releases].sort((a, b) => a.date.localeCompare(b.date) || byVersion(a.version, b.version));
const first = sorted[0];
const latest = sorted[sorted.length - 1];
const facts = [
  { label: 'First release', value: first ? `${first.version}, ${day(first.date)}` : 'none yet' },
  { label: 'Latest release', value: latest ? `${latest.version}, ${day(latest.date)}` : 'none yet' },
  { label: 'Licence', value: licence },
];
---
<Base title="About OpenTodo" description="Who makes OpenTodo, since when, what the licence means for you, how security is handled, where the specifications are, and how you can leave with your data at any time.">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">About and trust</p>
        <h1>Made in the open, for people who want their task list to be theirs.</h1>
        <p class="lede">A task app holds the next few years of your plans, so it is fair to ask who is behind it and what happens if they stop. This page answers that as plainly as we can, and links to the files where you can check it yourself.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts" aria-label="OpenTodo in numbers from the pinned release">
        {facts.map((x) => <div><dt>{x.label}</dt><dd>{x.value}</dd></div>)}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt" id="who" aria-labelledby="who-title">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <h2 id="who-title">Who makes it</h2>
        <p class="lede">OpenTodo is built and maintained by <a href={maintainer.url}>{maintainer.name}</a>, with contributions from people who use it. {maintainer.bio}</p>
        <p>It started as a replacement for subscription task apps for one household and grew from there. It is not a company, has no investors and sells nothing. Work happens in public on <a href={SITE.appRepo}>GitHub</a>: issues, pull requests, specifications and releases.</p>
        <p>And there is a Discord server where you can ask anything, share how you use OpenTodo and talk to the maintainer directly. You are very welcome there.</p>
        <div class="cta-row"><DiscordButton /></div>
      </div>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> <span>Every release is listed in the <a href={RELEASES_URL}>release notes</a> with what changed; the first was {first && day(first.date)}, the latest {latest && `${latest.version} on ${day(latest.date)}`}</span></li>
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> <span>Questions and conversation happen on <a href={SITE.discord}>the Discord server</a>. Bug reports and ideas go to <a href={`${SITE.appRepo}/issues`}>the issue tracker</a>; security reports go by email, see below</span></li>
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> The <a href="/blog/">blog</a> is written in the first person by the maintainer, dated and concrete</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="licence" aria-labelledby="licence-title">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The licence, in plain words</p>
        <h2 id="licence-title">{licence}: free to use, and it stays free</h2>
        <p class="lede">You can run OpenTodo for anything, study how it works, change it and share it. The one condition is that anyone who offers a changed version to other people over the network must publish their changes under the same licence.</p>
        <p>For you, that means three things. Nobody can paywall a feature you rely on. If the maintainer stopped tomorrow, the code, the container images and the <a href={`${SITE.appRepo}/tree/main/openspec/specs`}>specifications</a> would still be there for anyone to carry on. And the licence cannot be changed on the copy you already have.</p>
        <p><a class="link-arrow" href={`${SITE.appRepo}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>Read the licence <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></p>
        <p><a class="link-arrow" href="/support/">What it costs to keep OpenTodo free, and how you can help <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>What the licence does not do</h3>
        <p>It does not give anyone access to your server or your data. The licence is about the program's source code, not about what you keep in it. Your tasks are yours and <a href="/docs/import-export/">leave with you as one file</a> whenever you like.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt" id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Security</p>
        <h2 id="security-title">How your account and your data are protected</h2>
        <p class="lede">The security model is written down in the app's <a href={`${SITE.appRepo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>, which also says how to report a vulnerability privately by email. The short version:</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="key-round" size={22} /></span>
          <h3>Your account</h3>
          <p>Passwords are hashed, sign-up closes after the owner account, and you can add <a href="/docs/account-security/">passkeys and two-factor authentication</a>. Single sign-on with your own provider is optional.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="lock" size={22} /></span>
          <h3>Your tasks</h3>
          <p>They live on your server and nowhere else. If you want, <a href="/docs/e2ee/">end-to-end encryption</a> encrypts titles and notes on your devices, so not even the server can read them.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="eye-off" size={22} /></span>
          <h3>Nothing phones home</h3>
          <p>The app has no telemetry and makes no request to anyone but your server, unless you configure one: a backup destination, a webhook, or <a href="/docs/ai-and-agents/">an AI provider with your own key</a>.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="specs" aria-labelledby="specs-title">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Written down before it is built</p>
        <h2 id="specs-title">Every feature has a specification you can read</h2>
        <p class="lede">Each feature starts as a written proposal with testable scenarios, and only then becomes code. The specifications describe how OpenTodo behaves today, and they are public. If the documentation and the app ever disagree, the specification settles it.</p>
        <p><a class="link-arrow" href="/developers/#specs">The specifications, by topic <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><Icon name="history" size={20} /> The demo</h3>
        <p>The <a href={SITE.demo}>public demo</a> gives every visitor a private sandbox with sample data. Nobody else can see it, and it is deleted after two hours without activity and every night, so it is not a place to keep anything real. <a href="/docs/self-hosting/demo/">How the demo works</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt" id="this-site" aria-labelledby="site-title">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">This website</p>
        <h2 id="site-title">What this site says, and what it collects</h2>
        <p class="lede">Everything here about the app describes OpenTodo {versionTag}, the latest release: the version, the documentation, the screenshots and the release notes come from that release's own files, never from memory. Every statement about what the app can do links to its documentation page, and a build check fails when one does not.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> Comparisons name where other apps are ahead and show the date they were last checked</li>
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> Nothing is published without the maintainer's review; drafts arrive as pull requests on <a href={SITE.siteRepo}>the website's repository</a></li>
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> This website, and only this website, counts visits with self-hosted Umami: no cookies, no personal data, no third-party scripts. The app loads no analytics at all</li>
        <li><Icon name="check" size={18} /> The words and images here are <a href={`${SITE.siteRepo}/blob/main/LICENSE-CONTENT.md`}>CC BY 4.0</a>, the site's code is <a href={`${SITE.siteRepo}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="leaving" aria-labelledby="leaving-title">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="kicker">Leaving</p>
      <h2 id="leaving-title">You can go at any time, with everything</h2>
      <p class="lede">Every person on a server can <a href="/docs/import-export/">download all their data as one file</a> and import it on another OpenTodo server, or read it anywhere, since it is plain JSON. The server owner can take the whole data folder with them. Nothing keeps you here except that it works.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <EndCta />
</Base>
